1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:07,206
In a cheap power supply, one of the first parts to wear out is a small can called an electrolytic capacitor.

2
00:00:07,956 --> 00:00:11,103
These capacitors smooth the power your PC runs on.

3
00:00:11,383 --> 00:00:19,330
Each one is rated for a number of hours at a set temperature, like 5,000 hours at 105 degrees Celsius.

4
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:21,640
The rule of thumb is simple.

5
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:25,257
Every 10 degrees cooler roughly doubles that life.

6
00:00:25,537 --> 00:00:28,841
At 95 degrees, about 10,000 hours.

7
00:00:29,121 --> 00:00:31,331
At 85, about 20,000.

8
00:00:32,081 --> 00:00:33,648
It works the other way too.

9
00:00:33,928 --> 00:00:36,848
Run it hotter and its life shrinks just as fast.

10
00:00:37,598 --> 00:00:39,960
That's why cheap units tend to die early.

11
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:45,709
Smaller heatsinks and weaker fans let the capacitors run hotter, so they wear out sooner.

12
00:00:46,459 --> 00:00:50,226
Keep yours cool with clean dust filters and room to breathe.

13
00:00:50,506 --> 00:00:54,234
The full story is in our video, Power Supplies Explained.

